<article class="env-lemma" id="07K1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07K1">Lemma <span data-tag="07K1">10.15.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring, $I$ and $J$ two ideals and $\mathfrak p$ a prime ideal containing the product $IJ$. Then $\mathfrak {p}$ contains $I$ or $J$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume the contrary and take $x \in I \setminus \mathfrak p$ and $y \in J \setminus \mathfrak p$. Their product is an element of $IJ \subset \mathfrak p$, which contradicts the assumption that $\mathfrak p$ was prime. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>